{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are a CVE triage assistant. Input is JSON with `system_profile` (installed products and versions) and `advisories` (list of CVE IDs with description and affected info). Task: return exactly the CVE IDs from the supplied advisories that affect the installed system.\n\nOutput format: a single JSON object {\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]} — unique identifiers only, no duplicates, no extra keys or text. If none apply, return {\"applicable_cves\":[]}.\n\nMatching rules:\n1. Product match: An advisory applies only if the affected product is actually installed in the system profile. Product names may be formatted differently across the profile and advisories (e.g., underscores vs. spaces, vendor prefixes stripped, capitalization differences). Normalize names (lowercase, strip vendor prefixes, replace underscores with spaces) before comparing. Do not match advisories for products not installed.\n2. Version check: Only include an advisory if the installed version falls within the affected version range. Parse version strings as ordered numeric segments (e.g., x.y.z.w). Read the structured bounds carefully: some advisories specify fixed versions, others specify minimum/maximum bounds or exclusions. If the installed version is above the maximum affected bound, the advisory does NOT apply — exclude it. If it is below the minimum, exclude it. Compare all version segments, including fourth segments when present; do not stop after two or three segments. Pad shorter versions with zeros when comparing different lengths.\n3. Do not infer applicability from description text alone when structured version information contradicts it — the structured bounds take precedence.\n4. If an advisory mentions multiple products or multiple affected ranges, it applies if ANY installed product matches ANY affected range.\n\nCommon pitfalls to avoid:\n- Do not exclude an advisory merely because its description mentions a version that differs from the installed one; rely on the structured affected field.\n- Do not include an advisory for a product similar in name but not actually installed.\n- Do not include advisories whose affected range explicitly ends below the installed version (version_position above the bound ⇒ not applicable) or begins above it (version_position below the bound ⇒ not applicable).\n\nReturn only the JSON object, nothing else."},"handle":"b8fe31eeb75b05b7b6cf097966897ea833dacac6d15f5631e90162d600002c09","profile_ids":["optimization_0069","optimization_0234","optimization_0013","optimization_0010","optimization_0227","optimization_0176","optimization_0177","optimization_0210","optimization_0067","optimization_0040","optimization_0011","optimization_0038","optimization_0111","optimization_0132","optimization_0043","optimization_0112","optimization_0209","optimization_0062","optimization_0093","optimization_0120","optimization_0007","optimization_0226","optimization_0083","optimization_0230"],"rows":[{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded"],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":2,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2023-25615","CVE-2023-26457"],"profile_id":"optimization_0069","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded","version_excluded"],"expected":["CVE-2023-40153","CVE-2024-54157","CVE-2024-54158"],"fn":0,"fp":3,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2023-38068","CVE-2023-40153","CVE-2024-38506","CVE-2024-47162","CVE-2024-54157","CVE-2024-54158"],"profile_id":"optimization_0234","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":3,"valid":true},{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded"],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":2,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2023-4934","CVE-2024-9110"],"profile_id":"optimization_0013","status":"valid","stratum":"medium","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":["missed_applicable","missed_applicable"],"expected":["CVE-2023-21521","CVE-2023-21522","CVE-2023-39219","CVE-2024-22377","CVE-2024-